  "prediction": "**Wildlife Trafficking: Review of U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service and National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration's Use of Financial Rewards**\n\nThe U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service (FWS) and the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) have laws that authorize the payment of financial rewards for information on wildlife trafficking. However, the agencies have not prioritized the use of rewards and were unable to provide sufficient assurance that the rewards they paid from fiscal years 2007 through 2017 represented all of the rewards they provided during that period.\n\nFWS and NOAA have policies outlining the processes for making reward payments, but FWS's policy does not specify factors for agents to consider when developing proposed reward amounts. The agencies have also communicated little information to the public on the potential availability of rewards, which may deter people with information from coming forward.\n\nThe report recommends that FWS and NOAA track financial reward information, augment their policies to specify factors for agents to consider when developing proposed reward amounts, and communicate additional information to the public on rewards. The agencies should also review the effectiveness of their use of financial rewards and implement any changes that would improve their usefulness in combating wildlife trafficking.\n\nThe report also notes that FWS and NOAA have not reviewed the effectiveness of their use of financial rewards or considered whether any changes might improve the usefulness of rewards as a law enforcement tool. By undertaking such reviews, the agencies can identify opportunities to improve the usefulness of rewards in combating wildlife trafficking.\n\nThe report makes seven recommendations to FWS and NOAA, including tracking financial reward information, augmenting policies, and reviewing the effectiveness of their use of financial rewards. The Department of Commerce and the Department of the Interior concurred with the recommendations, and NOAA outlined plans to implement them.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the need for FWS and NOAA to improve their use of financial rewards in combating wildlife trafficking and to provide more information to the public on the potential availability of rewards.",
